  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-3+4\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-3-4\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-3+4\\text{i}))(x-(-3-4\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-3-4\\text{i})-x(-3+4\\text{i})+(-3+4\\text{i})(-3-4\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+4x\\text{i}+3x-4x\\text{i}+9-16{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-3\\pm 4\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+6x+25) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+18x+75 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
